      "body": "I reviewed `pruefer/claude.go`, `pruefer/review.go`, `pruefer/findings.go`, `github/prs.go`, `github/types.go`, and the accompanying tests/ADR for this PR (fetching prior review threads and feeding them into the review prompt, plus intra-pass finding deduplication). The implementation is well-tested, `go build`/`go vet`/`go test -race ./pruefer/... ./github/...` are clean, and the code matches the stated design fairly closely (degrade-not-fail thread fetch, `(Path, Line)` dedup keying reused from `partitionFindings`, ordered/capped thread rendering). Two real gaps stood out: a silent truncation of long review-thread comment histories that undermines the \"never silently drop\" principle the PR itself establishes for the thread cap, and a widened prompt-injection surface now that arbitrary (including non-collaborator, on public repos) review-comment bodies flow verbatim into the Claude prompt.",
